;">PLUS</span></div></button></div></div><div class="flex basis-auto flex-col -mb-(--composer-overlap-px) [--composer-overlap-px:24px] grow overflow-hidden"><div class="relative h-full"><div class="flex h-full flex-col overflow-y-auto [scrollbar-gutter:stable_both-edges] @[84rem]/thread:pt-(--header-height)"><div aria-hidden="true" data-edge="true" class="pointer-events-none h-px w-px"></div><div class="@thread-xl/thread:pt-header-height mt-1.5 flex flex-col text-sm pb-25"><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full" dir="auto" data-testid="conversation-turn-1" data-scroll-anchor="false"><h5 class="sr-only">You said:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto py-5 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto flex max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 text-base gap-4 md:gap-5 lg:gap-6 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden" tabindex="-1"><div class="group/conversation-turn relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col"><div class="relative flex-col gap-1 md:gap-3"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="bbb21543-91df-4055-bd83-7deddd564e87" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="relative max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)] bg-token-message-surface rounded-3xl px-5 py-2.5"><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">Surveillance capitalism, according to Shoshana Zuboff, is a novel economic system and form of capitalist accumulation that exploits personal human experience as raw material for data extraction and behavioral prediction. Zuboff coined the term in 2014 to describe how large technology companies, pioneered by Google and later Facebook, unilaterally claim private human experience to transform it into "behavioral surplus"-data that goes beyond what is needed for service improvement and is instead used to create "prediction products" sold in new "behavioral futures markets" for profit[1][2][5].

Key aspects of Zuboff's definition include:

- **Extraction and commodification of personal data:** Surveillance capitalism involves mass collection and monetization of individuals' raw personal data, often without meaningful consent, to predict and modify behavior for commercial gain[2][5].

- **Behavioral futures markets:** The data collected is processed using machine intelligence to anticipate and influence what individuals will do now and in the future, creating a new marketplace for these behavioral predictions[5][6].

- **Dispossession by surveillance:** Zuboff describes this as a "coup from above," where individuals lose control over their personal information and autonomy, as rights become concentrated in the hands of surveillance capitalists[2].

- **Instrumentarian power:** Unlike traditional capitalism that exploits natural resources or labor, surveillance capitalism exploits human nature itself, using "instrumentarian" methods designed to observe and modify behavior without the individual's awareness or consent[4].

- **Undermining democracy and autonomy:** Zuboff warns that surveillance capitalism threatens core societal values such as freedom, democracy, and privacy by creating a pervasive digital architecture ("Big Other") that operates without democratic oversight and controls human behavior for profit[1][2][6].

- **New logic of accumulation:** This system is radically disembedded from traditional capitalism, relying on continuous data extraction, personalized services, automated contracts, and ongoing behavioral experiments on users[2].

In summary, Shoshana Zuboff defines surveillance capitalism as a new, powerful economic order where corporations extract and commodify personal data to predict and shape human behavior, undermining individual autonomy and democratic institutions while generating vast wealth through behavioral futures markets[1][2][5][6].

