<h3 data-start="1944" data-end="1974" class=""><strong data-start="1948" data-end="1974">Systemic Intersections</strong></h3>
<ol data-start="1976" data-end="2716">
<li data-start="1976" data-end="2223" class="">
<p data-start="1979" data-end="2223" class=""><strong data-start="1979" data-end="2012">Data-as-Weapon Infrastructure</strong>: Surveillance capitalism provides the infrastructure (geolocation, biometric, psychometric data) increasingly used in <strong data-start="2131" data-end="2158">necrocapitalist domains</strong> like predictive policing, migrant tracking, or targeted warfare.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2225" data-end="2468" class="">
<p data-start="2228" data-end="2468" class=""><strong data-start="2228" data-end="2252">Platform Sovereignty</strong>: Chokepoint capitalism enforces <strong data-start="2285" data-end="2326">informational and economic dependency</strong>, amplifying both surveillance (via mandatory data trails) and necropolitics (e.g., gig workers‚Äô exposure to economic death without recourse).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2470" data-end="2716" class="">
<p data-start="2473" data-end="2716" class=""><strong data-start="2473" data-end="2502">Algorithmic Disposability</strong>: All three systems can render individuals <strong data-start="2545" data-end="2572">epistemically invisible</strong> or ontologically redundant‚Äîwhether as non-profitable users (surveillance), non-compliant workers (chokepoint), or non-marketable lives (necro).</p>
</li>
</ol>
