<hr data-start="328" data-end="331" class="">
<h2 data-start="333" data-end="420" class=""><strong data-start="336" data-end="420">Appendix: Quadratic Program Relaxations (QPRs) for Triadic Negentropic Cognition</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="422" data-end="438" class=""><strong data-start="426" data-end="438">Overview</strong></h3>
<p data-start="440" data-end="817" class=""><strong data-start="440" data-end="480">Quadratic Program Relaxations (QPRs)</strong> represent soft optimization motifs embedded in recursive cognitive-symbolic systems. Unlike constrained programs, QPRs do not enforce hard feasibility but instead allow <strong data-start="650" data-end="694">gradient-shaped, energy-based relaxation</strong> of competing drives. Each type of QPR corresponds to a negentropic strategy employed by one or more of the triadic agents:</p>
<ul data-start="819" data-end="977">
<li data-start="819" data-end="868" class="">
<p data-start="821" data-end="868" class=""><strong data-start="821" data-end="837">Psychocinema</strong> (affective-symbolic recursion)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="869" data-end="927" class="">
<p data-start="871" data-end="927" class=""><strong data-start="871" data-end="896">Geometric Bayesianism</strong> (epistemic manifold reasoning)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="928" data-end="977" class="">
<p data-start="930" data-end="977" class=""><strong data-start="930" data-end="943">SpherePop</strong> (syntactic-expression navigation)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="979" data-end="982" class="">
<h2 data-start="984" data-end="1021" class=""><strong data-start="987" data-end="1021">Five QPR Types and Their Roles</strong></h2>
